{-
   Reading a clock, on the target that has one - Analysis-Library.md §3.1.

   `Clock.native.yana` beside this is the same two names over `clock_gettime`, and `Date.yana` above
   both is portable because of it. What the two files share is the pair of names and nothing else,
   which is Analysis-Modules.md §2.5's rule for what earns a file rather than an attribute.

   **There is one clock here, and it is the wall clock.** `Date.now()` is the only time source every
   host agrees on: `performance.now()` exists in browsers and in Node, and it is deliberately *not*
   used. Its resolution is clamped by the engine as a side-channel mitigation - a millisecond in
   several browsers by default, and coarser still where the document is not cross-origin isolated -
   so it is not reliably better than what is here; its time origin is per-document rather than
   per-process, so two readings taken in different frames are not comparable; and it is absent from
   a bare script context, which is exactly what this project's own JavaScript driver runs
   (`vm.runInNewContext` supplies the ECMAScript built-ins and nothing else). A declaration a host
   may not have is a program that stops working on it, and this is a library.

   So `monotonicNanos` below is *not* monotonic here, and the comment on it says so rather than
   letting the name claim otherwise. On this target it steps whenever the machine's clock is
   corrected, and it resolves to a millisecond. What it is still good for is measuring something
   that takes longer than one, which is most of what a script measures.
-}

-- `Date.now()`, as the integral milliseconds it answers. A `number` becomes a `Long` here and the
-- arithmetic below is done in it: a millisecond count is about 2^41 today, so microseconds still
-- fit a double exactly and *nanoseconds do not* - 2^61 - and one conversion for both is simpler than
-- an argument per unit about which side of it is exact.
fn unixMillis() -> I64:
    let milliseconds = truncate(hostDateNow()) :: Long
    return milliseconds :: I64

-- Microseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC, which is what `Timestamp` holds.
pub fn unixTimeMicros() -> I64 = unixMillis() * 1000

-- The same clock again, in the unit `Clock.native.yana` promises. Not monotonic here - see above -
-- and resolved to a millisecond, so the last six digits are always zero.
pub fn monotonicNanos() -> I64 = unixMillis() * 1000000
